Year = {2024},
Volume = {36},
Number = {1},
Pages = {89-105},
Abstract = {Japan has always been prone to earthquakes, as the country is located in
   a zone of high seismic activity known as the Pacific Ring of Fire. The
   unique Japanese sense of nature, which is also part of national
   tradition, stands alongside the constant threat of earthquakes from
   ancient times to the present. This paper will analyze the occurrence of
   earthquakes in Japan, emphasizing the relationship between nature,
   non-human world and humanity, and their representations in mythology and
   folklore, with reference to the most recent devastating earthquake, the
   Fukushima disaster (2011).},
